<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Robert Baldwin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1804">1804</a>–<a href="/years/1858">1858</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Robert Baldwin was a lawyer and Reform politician of Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and Canada West, the leading advocate of responsible government. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-hippolyte-lafontaine">Louis-Hippolyte LaFontaine</a> he led the ministry of 1848 to 1851, which established that the cabinet must keep the confidence of the elected assembly. His government also reformed municipal institutions and turned King's College into the secular University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/robert-baldwin.jpg" alt="Robert Baldwin" width="320" height="398" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Miscellaneous Collection/Library and Archives Canada/C-031493 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertBaldwin23.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Baldwin came of a prominent family: his grandfather, also Robert Baldwin, moved to Upper Canada from Ireland in 1799, and his father was William Warren Baldwin, to whom with his son the idea of responsible government has been attributed. In 1827 he married his cousin Augusta Elizabeth Sullivan, with whom he had two sons and two daughters; her death in 1836 ended the marriage. Described as melancholy and awkward in public, he won a by-election for the town of York in 1830 but lost his seat in the general election later that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1836 the new Lieutenant Governor, Sir Francis Bond Head, appointed Baldwin to the Executive Council; when Bond Head would not confirm in writing a commitment to responsible government, Baldwin resigned within a month and persuaded the other councillors to go with him. Bond Head then won an election marked by Orange Order violence. Baldwin disapproved of the rebellion of 1837, carried a flag of truce to the rebel camp north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a> on 5 December 1837, and with his father advised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-lambton-1st-earl-of-durham">Lord Durham</a> to recommend responsible government.</p><p>Named Solicitor General in 1840, he was elected for 4th York and Hastings. Orange violence kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-hippolyte-lafontaine">Louis-Hippolyte LaFontaine</a> from a seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada-east">Canada East</a>, so Baldwin had him nominated for 4th York; when Baldwin lost Hastings, LaFontaine arranged his election at Rimouski in 1843. Their first ministry, formed in September 1842, ended in November 1843 in a year-long crisis with Governor Sir Charles Metcalfe, who rejected Baldwin's Secret Societies Bill against the Orange Order. Baldwin founded a Reform Association in February 1844.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-bruce-8th-earl-of-elgin">Lord Elgin</a> was sent to accept responsible government, and the second Baldwin-LaFontaine administration, formed on 11 March 1848, passed the Amnesty Act, the Baldwin Act on municipal government and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-losses-bill">Rebellion Losses Bill</a> of 1849, after which rioters burned the Parliament buildings in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. Baldwin resigned in 1851 after most Upper Canada members backed abolishing the Court of Chancery, and he lost York after refusing a pledge on the Clergy Reserves. He died at Spadina on 9 December 1858. John Ralston Saul credited Baldwin and LaFontaine with Canada's non-violent political tradition.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Robert Baldwin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Reformers <b>Robert Baldwin</b> and Louis-Hippolyte LaFontaine achieved responsible government in 1848, confirmed when Lord Elgin signed the Rebellion Losses Bill despite riots that burned Montreal's parliament in 1849.</blockquote><a href="/state/province-of-canada">Province of Canada</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toronto">Toronto</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/canada">Canada</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=43.67,-79.39&amp;year=1839" aria-label="Toronto on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2642 616 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Robert Baldwin"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3092 886h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3092 886h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=43.67,-79.39&amp;year=1839">What was here in 1839? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
